    <h1>Peptide COA red flags: 8 signs of a recycled certificate</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">A COA is easy to produce and hard to verify. These are the patterns that separate a real release document from a template.</p>

    <h2>1. No batch number, or a batch that does not match the label</h2>
    <p>The certificate must tie to the physical lot. If the number differs from the vial label or invoice, the document proves nothing about what you received.</p>

    <h2>2. Purity without a chromatogram</h2>
    <p>A percentage alone is unverifiable. Real release data includes the trace, integration and impurity profile.</p>

    <h2>3. The same file across products</h2>
    <p>Identical layout is normal; identical peak retention times, dates and signatures across different compounds are not.</p>

    <h2>4. Methods described as "in-house" with no detail</h2>
    <p>Column, gradient and wavelength are not trade secrets — they are what makes a result reproducible.</p>

    <h2>5. No mass-spec identity</h2>
    <p>HPLC proves homogeneity, not identity. Without MS, "the right peak" could be the wrong molecule.</p>

    <h2>6. Water content marked "N/A"</h2>
    <p>Moisture affects stability and weighing. A serious release document reports it.</p>

    <h2>7. Certification logos with no numbers</h2>
    <p>ISO or GMP marks without a certificate number and issuing body cannot be verified. Treat them as decoration.</p>

    <h2>8. Results that are too clean</h2>
    <p>Real synthesis leaves minor peaks. A chromatogram with no visible impurities at all deserves a second look at the integration.</p>

    <h2>What to do</h2>
    <p>Ask for the raw chromatogram file, the MS spectrum and the batch record reference. A supplier with a real system answers in minutes; one without changes the subject.</p>

